Highly Efficient Conversion and Reaction Mechanism of Propargylic Amines and $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ Catalyzed by Eco-Friendly NobleMetal-Free [Zn116] Nanocages
## Introduction
Carbon dioxide $(\mathsf{C O}_{2})$ , as waste gas, has been accumulating in the atmosphere at an alarming pace, causing serious environmental problems like ocean acidification and global warming.1 Therefore, the chemical fixation or conversion of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ has become one of the most urgent themes in $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ chemistry research field.2 This kind of method not only reduces the atmospheric greenhouse gas, but also generates various high value-added chemicals, such as cyclic carbonates,3 dimethyl carbonate,4 formic acid,5 N,N'-disubstituted ureas,6 and others.7 Among those products, 2-oxazolidinones which can be synthesized by the reaction of propargylic amines with $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , have received considerable attention due to their wide applications in organic synthesis, chiral auxiliaries and antibacterial pharmaceuticals.8 However, due to the $c=0$ bonds in ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ owning high bond enthalpy $(+805\mathrm{\kJ\cdotmol^{-1}})$ and stable $\boldsymbol{\uppi}_{3}^{4}$ electronic structure, various highly active catalysts are necessary and important to promote the reaction. Presently, various catalysts have been employed to accelerate the reaction, such as organocatalysts,9 transition metal catalysts,10 and etc.11 Furthermore, a few MOFs-based catalysts for conversion of propargylic amines with $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ show remarkable cycle performance, like Ag-based MOFs of TNS-Ag8 and TOS-Ag4, a $\mathsf{A g}(1)$ -decorated sulfonate-MOF, a $\mathsf{A g}(|)$ -anchored pyrazolatebased MOF of TMOF-3-Ag, and a Cd-MOF.12 Nevertheless, it's worth mentioning that most of these catalysts are associated with expensive noble-metal or environmentally hazardous heavy metal. To our knowledge, it has never been reported that ecofriendly noble-metal-free MOFs catalysts can catalyze the cyclization of propargylic amines with $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ .
On the other hand, metal clusters, as a hot research issue, are widely employed for fabricating metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) not only because of the corresponding fascinating structures, but also their extensively promising applications, especially in catalytic aspects.13 Actually, discrete metal clusters have exhibited excellent catalytic properties in previous explorations, and organic linkers bridging metal clusters into nano-cages in MOFs could greatly promote the catalytic efficiency through confinement effect.14 Thus, nano-cages in MOFs could be defined as nano-catalytic reactors, and the catalytic efficiency is directly related to the number of catalytic sites, namely metal clusters. Up to now, many nano-cages in MOFs have been explored and reported, such as ZIF-8, MIL-101, UiO-66, etc.15 But comparably, only a few examples about cagebased MOFs with high-nuclear Zn-clusters as secondary building units (SBUs) have been documented and impressed. For example, $[Z\mathsf{n}_{24}]$ cage constructed by six $Z n_{4}O.$ -blocks,16 [Zn36] cage built by nine $Z n_{4}O$ clusters,17 [Zn48] cage assembled by mixed-cluster units,18 [Zn72] cage via the assembly of eighteen $[Z n_{4}\mathrm{O}]$ clusters,19 and large $[Z\mathsf{n}_{104}]$ cage has been also obtained by twenty-four $Z n_{4}O$ clusters.20 However, to our knowledge, a high-nuclear [Znx] $(\mathsf{x}>104)$ cage assembled by Zn-clusters has never been reported hitherto.
In this contribution, a novel 3D framework $[Z\mathsf{n}_{22}(\mathsf{T r}\mathsf{z})_{8}(\mathsf{O H})_{12}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{9}\cdot8\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}]_{\mathsf{n}}$ ${\sf T r Z}=({\sf C}_{4}{\sf N}_{12}{\sf O})^{4-}$ (1) assembled by giant $[Z n_{116}]$ nanocages was prepared through in situ $[2+3]$ cycloaddition reaction, representing the highest nuclearity nanocages assembled by Zn-clusters as building blocks (Figure 1). The potassium tricyanomethanide $(\mathsf{K C}_{4}\mathsf{N}_{3})$ was employed as precursor ligand to react with $N a N_{3}$ under the catalysis of $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O$ to construct the interesting 3D MOF, containing in situ generated ligand tri(1H-tetrazol-5-yl)methanol $(H_{4}T r z)$ as shown in Scheme S1. In the Demko-Sharpless reaction,21 organic nitrile and azide salt can go on $[2+3]$ cycloaddition reaction with the catalysis of zinc salts, which is a safe and environmental method to synthesize tetrazole. 1 exhibits high thermo-stability, solvent-stability and pH-stability. The catalytic investigations reveals that compound 1 can act as an efficient catalyst for propargylic amines conversion with $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ into 2- oxazolidinones, and the stable framework can be reused at least ten times without any obvious loss in catalytic activity. Importantly, it is the first eco-friendly noble-metal-free MOFs catalyst for the cyclization of propargylic amines with $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ . DFT calculations, NMR and FTIR monitoring exhibit that $[Z n_{116}]$ can play an important role in effectively activing $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and $C\equiv0$ bonds of propargylic amines.
## Results and Discussion
Single-crystal X-ray analysis manifests that compound 1 crystallizes in a tetragonal crystal system with space group $14/m$ (Table S1). Each asymmetric unit consists of four crystallographically independent Zn ions (Figure 1a). The coordination environment of $Z_{\mathsf{n}1}$ is furnished by three N (N1, N5C and N12A) and two O (O1A and O1C) atoms. Zn2 is sixcoordinated, binding four N (N7, N7A, N7B and N7C) and two water molecules (O2 and O3). Zn3 links with two water molecules (O6 and O7) and four O (O4, O4D, O5 and O5A) atoms from $\mu{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{O}\mathsf{H}$ . The coordination geometry of Zn4 is completed by two N (N8 and N9) and two O (O4 and O4A) atoms from $\mu{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{O}{\mathsf{H}}$ , as well O2 interacts weakly with Zn4 with $2\mathsf{n}4^{\cdots}\mathsf{O}2$ distance of $2.638\textsf{A}$ Five $Z n(Z n2,Z n4,Z n4A,Z n4B$ and $Z n4C)$ ) ions assemble into the tetragonal pyramidal Zn5 unit (Figure 1b and Figure S1), and four Zn (Zn3, Zn3A, Zn3B and Zn3C) ions in the same plane build the square Zn4 unit. Interestingly, one Zn4 and two Zn5 units form a sandwich-like tetrahexahedron $[Z{\mathsf{n}}_{14}{\mathsf{O}}_{21}]$ cluster (Figure S1), and each $[Z n_{14}\mathsf{O}_{21}]$ cluster coordinates with eight ${\mathsf{T r}}2^{4-}$ ligands (Figure 1c). Four Zn (Zn1, Zn1A, Zn1B and Zn1C) ions constitute a $[Z n_{4}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}]$ cluster by connecting four ${\mathsf{T r}}Z^{4-}$ anions (Figure 1d). Eight $[Z n_{4}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}]$ clusters as the vertices can form a slightly distorted cube with the edge of $1.1\ \mathsf{n m}$ , and each surface of this cube is capped by one $[Z\mathsf{n}_{14}\mathsf{O}_{21}]$ cluster (Figure S2). Thus, six $[Z{\mathsf{n}}_{14}{\mathsf{O}}_{21}]$ clusters and eight $[Z n_{4}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}]$ clusters are bridged by thirty-two ${\mathsf{T r}}2^{4-}$ anions, assembling into the giant lantern-like $[Z n_{116}]$ nanocage (Figures 1e and 1f). The unique cage has a large cavity of ca. $0.81\times1.03$ nm and the external edge ca. $2.37\times3.65\:\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S3). To our knowledge, it is the highest nuclearity $Z n$ -based nanocages constructed by $Z n$ -clusters as building blocks, although several impressive large Zn-cages like [Zn48], [Zn72] and [Zn104] were well documented.18-20 Furthermore, the [Zn116] nanocages assemble into 3D framework (Figure 1g), possessing solvent accessible volumes of $34.8\%$ calculated by the PLATON program.22 From the topological point of view, each $[Z n_{4}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}]$ cluster linking with four ligands can be considered as a 4- connected node, and the $[Z{\mathsf{n}}_{14}{\mathsf{O}}_{21}]$ cluster bonding with eight ligands can be regarded as 8-connected node. Therefore, the framework can be designated as a binodal (4,8)-connected net with flu topology (Figure 1h), and Schläfli symbol for net is $\{4^{12}{\cdot}6^{12}{\cdot}8^{4}\}\{4^{6}\}_{2}$ . The tiling representation of [Zn116] cages in compound 1 is shown in Figure S4.
Powder X-Ray diffraction (PXRD) of compound 1 was measured (Figure S5). The almost identical PXRD patterns between the synthesized and simulated one reveal the high purity of compound 1. The stability of compound 1 was further explored. The samples of compound 1 were immersed in eight common solvents for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , and the measured PXRD patterns still remain consistent with the simulated one (Figure S6). The samples were immersed in a series of solutions with pH values from 1 to 14 for $4\ h$ , and the framework remains unchanged under ${\mathsf{p H}}=2{-}12$ (Figure S7). However, under $p\mathsf{H}=1$ or 14, some peaks gradually weaken or disappear, indicating that the samples lose the long-range ordering crystalline state. While a new peak appears in PXRD of $\mathsf{p H}=13$ , it means the forming of new phases. Thus, compound 1 can keep stable in solutions with ${\mathsf{p H}}=2{-}12$ at least for $4h$ . The thermo-gravimetric analyse (TGA) was also carried out under ${\sf N}_{2}$ atmosphere, revealing that the framework of 1 can remain intact until about $327^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (Figure S8). $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption of compound 1 was investigated (Figure S9), and adsorption isotherm at 298 K exhibits hysteresis with $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ uptake of $27.00~\mathsf{c m}^{3}/\mathsf{g}$ at ambient pressure, indicating that porous framework possesses the capacity of capturing ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ .
Zn-clusters in the compound 1 can act as Lewis acidic sites, offering the promising application for the carboxylative cyclization of propargylic amines with $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ . A series of propargylic amines were prepared according to the published procedures.10a,11 The detailed preparation procedures are represented in Schemes S2 and S3, followed by the characterization of substrates and products. The cyclization of N-benzylprop-2-yn-1-amine and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ was selected as a model reaction, and reaction conditions screening is summarized in Table S2. No target product was detected without catalyst 1 or any additives (entry 1 of Table S2). When only catalyst 1 was employed without additives, the presence of solvent had no obvious effect on the reaction (entries 2 vs. 3 of Table ${\mathsf{S}}2$ ). Taking account of the use of bases contributing to shift the equilibrium from propargylic amines and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ to the carbamate anion,23 a series of bases were screened (entries 4-9 of Table S2), as anticipated, activated 1 and the bases markedly had influence on reaction outcome. When tBuOLi was employed, the corresponding product 2a was obtained only in $3\%$ yield (entry 4), which may be attributed to low solubility of tBuOLi. Compared with an insoluble inorganic base, N-donor organic bases with good solubility have a significantly effect on accelerating the cyclization reaction.23 Intriguingly, the addition of imidazole results in low yield of 2a (entry 5 of Table S2). The finding may be interpreted that the chelation of imidazole and $Z n^{2+}$ may restrict the combination of $Z n^{2+}$ and substrates. DABCO (1,4-diazabicyclo-[2.2.2]-octane) is confirmed to be little effect (entry 6 of Table S2). Guanidines are good choice for the synthesis of carbamates not only due to their basicity but also in virtue of the ability to stabilize bidentate anions like carbamates.24 Remarkably, DBU (1,8-diazabicyclo-[5.4.0]- undec-7-ene) showed better influence on enhancing catalytic activity of 1 than DABCO (entries 7 vs. 6 of Table S2). Then guanidines TMG (1,1,3,3-tetramethyl-guanidine) and TBD were selected to catalyze the reaction (entries 8 vs. 9 of Table S2). To our delight, TMG and TBD displayed excellent behavior for catalyzing the carboxylative cyclization to produce 2a in $94\%$ and $99\%$ yields, respectively. The reaction in the absence of solvent still obtained an excellent yield up to $87\%$ (entries 11 vs.
9 of Table S2). The influence of temperature (25, 50 and $70\ {}^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{)}}$ on the reaction was investigated (entries 9, 12 and 13 of Table S2), and the reaction at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ obtained the higher yield. The influence of reaction time was also tested. As a result, the reaction could accomplish $96\%$ in 9 h (entry 14 of Table S2). From entry 15 of Table S2, we can draw a conclusion that the reducing of TBD vastly decreased the yield. $10\ m g$ catalyst 1 can also effectively catalyze the conversion (entry 16 of Table S2). However, 1a could not completely transform into 2a with 5 mg catalyst 1 (entry 17 of Table S2). In conclusion, the optimum conditions are 1a ( $145.2\mathrm{mg},1\mathrm{mmol})$ , catalyst 1 (10 mg, 0.0027 mmol, $0.27\mathrm{\mol}\%$ relative to 1a), TBD (0.2 mmol, $20\mathrm{\mol\%}$ relative to 1a), $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ balloon, $70~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , 12 h and $0.5~\mathsf{m L}$ $C H_{3}C N$ (entry 16 of Table S2).
For the purpose of further exploring and verifying the catalytic potential of 1, the reactivity of various propargylic amines was examined under the optimized conditions (Table 1). The carboxylative cyclization can be extended to multiple N-aryl or N-alkyl substituted terminal propargylic amines, giving the corresponding yields of $58-99\%$ (entries 1-8 of Table 1). The model reaction of 1a exhibited the highest reactivity to $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ with $99\%$ yield of 2a (entry 1 of Table 1). However, introduction of the methyl of N-aryl substituted group can increase the steric hindrance resulting in the corresponding 2-oxazolidinone in moderate yield (entry 2 of Table 1). When phenethyl substituent group replaced benzyl, the yield was $91\%$ (entry 3 of Table 1). Unfortunately, N-phenyl-prop-2-yn-1-amine (1d) manifested a low reactivity (entry 4 of Table 1). Notably, the carboxylative cyclization approach proceeded with appreciable yields when propargylic amines derivatives bearing either electron-donating (-OCH3) or electron-withdrawing (-Cl) groups were used (entries 5 and 6 of Table 1). Terminal propargylic amines with n-butyl or cyclohexyl substituent at N-position can be also transformed into the desired products in high yields (entries 7 and 8 of Table 1). It is understandable that N-butyl-1-phenylpent-1-yn-3-amine (1i) displayed moderate reactivity.
In terms of the practical application, the recyclable performance of heterogeneous catalysts is an important and indispensable parameter. Considering catalyst 1 can be easily separated from the reaction mixture by centrifugation and filtration, the recycle performance of catalyst 1 was tested as the model reaction. As depicted in Figure 2, the catalyst can be reused at least ten times without any obvious decrease in catalytic efficiency, manifesting that 1 has the excellent recyclability. The PXRD patterns after recycling processes show that the PXRD of 1 after six cycles are still well consistent with the original one, thus 1 still keeps crystalline state, while some peaks after runs 7-10 gradually broaden and/or weaken until disappear, suggesting that the long-range ordering crystalline state of 1 gradually becomes poor and transform into amorphous state after being drastically stirred (Figure S10). Inductively coupled plasma (ICP) measurement of reaction filtrate reveals that only $<2\%$ leakage of $Z n^{2+}$ was tested after five times, while the leak amount increased a little with the recycling, which can be attributed to a small quantity of 1 have transformed into amorphous state or decomposed after drastically stirring for long time (Table S3). In order to explore the nature of the reaction, catalyst filtration test was confirmed (Figure S11), after removal of catalyst at $3h$ of the reaction, the transformation of propargylic amine dramatically decreased, which suggests that compound 1 is a heterogeneous catalyst.
However, catalytic experiment continued to react slowly may result from a little leakage of $Z n^{2+}$ .
[a]All the reactions were carried out with propargylic amines (1 mmol) and catalyst 1 (10 mg, 0.0027 mmol, $0.27\mathrm{\mol}\%$ relative to the substrate 1a under atmospheric pressure of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ at $70^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ within $12\mathsf{h}$ 0.5 mL $C H_{3}C N$ . [b]Determined by $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR with 1,3,5- trimethoxybenzene as internal standard.
To explore the catalytic mechanism of 1, the interactions between propargylic amines and catalyst were monitored by $^1\mathsf{H}$ NMR and $\mathsf{\Omega}^{13}\mathsf{C}$ NMR spectroscopy (Figure 3 and Figure S12). After adding 1, $^1\mathsf{H}$ signal of $C\equiv C-H$ $(\mathsf{d}~=~2.64$ ppm) in 1a becomes a broad and dwarf peak, doubtlessly demonstrating that $C\equiv0$ bond can be activated by catalyst 1 (Figure 3). But $\mathsf{\Omega}^{13}\mathsf{C}$ signal change in $C\equiv0$ bond is not been obviously observed. Additionally, it is worth mentioning that $^{13}\mathsf{C}$ signal of TBD $\langle{\mathsf{d}}=$ 151.34 or $23.73~\mathsf{p p m}.$ disappear or weaken after adding catalyst 1, which may indicate the interactions between TBD and catalyst 1 (Figure S12). As shown in Figures S13 and S14, the characteristic peaks in 1a gradually decrease and disappear at last with the increasing time, while the new characteristic peaks in 2a appear and arise gradually. Fourier-transformed infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) was employed to further confirm the activation of propargylic amine molecules by $Z n(11)$ sites (Figure S15). Catalyst $1$ after being treated with 1a and washed with ethanol, shows characteristic peaks of $\tt\equiv C\cdot H$ stretching at 3290 cm-1 and very weak sign of $C\equiv0$ stretching at $2105~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ , confirming the adsorption of the substrates in the pores. Compared with the $\tt\equiv C\cdot H$ stretching vibration peak of 1a, the corresponding characteristic peak of $\tt\equiv C\cdot H$ stretching becomes broad and dwarf, which indicates the interactions between 1a and catalyst 1.
Based on the above results and reported works,10a,11 density functional theory (DFT) calculations are further performed to propose a possible reaction mechanism for fixation of propargylic amines with $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ (Scheme 1). The optimized geometric structure (S0) of compound 1 is shown in Figure S16. and Zn3 (Zn3, Zn3A, Zn3B and $Z n3C)$ ions are the active sites for the spatial convenience and higher charge of $+1.317$ e. The detailed progresses are given as following: 1) The $\mathsf{H}^{+}$ of hydroxyl coordinating to Zn3 combines with TBD to form $\boldsymbol{[\mathsf{T B D H}]^{+}}$ with substantial energy reduction of $2.738~\mathsf{e V}$ , which indicates the formation of $[\mathsf{T B D H}]^{+}$ should be efficient and favorable in thermodynamics (Figure S17). The result is consistent with $^{13}\mathsf{C}$ NMR spectroscopy changes of TBD after adding catalyst 1 in Figure S12. The framework loses $\mathsf{H}^{+}$ to form S1 with charge of - 0.886 e (Figure S18), and $Z n3–05$ bond length shortens from 2.014 to 1.973 Å. 2) $C_{\mathsf{a}}\equiv\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{b}}$ bond in propargylic amine can be activated by $z_{\mathsf{n3}}$ based on exchanging with coordinated $H_{2}O$ , forming S2 (Figure S19) and releasing energy of 0.271 eV. In structure S2, $\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{a}}$ and ${\mathsf{C}}_{\mathsf{b}}$ atoms of $C_{\mathsf{a}}\equiv\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{b}}$ bond link with Zn3 with the distances of 2.133 $(Z n3–C_{a})$ and 2.657 $(Z_{n}3–C_{\mathrm{6}})$ Å. The sandwich-like tetrahexahedron $[Z{\mathsf{n}}_{14}{\mathsf{O}}_{21}]$ cluster distorts with the distance of 2.083 Å (Zn3-O5). Due to the high covalent component of $Z n3–C_{a}$ bond, the charge of $z_{\mathsf{n3}}$ further reduces to $+1.214\textsf{e}$ , which is lower than those $(+1.295$ , $+1.347$ and $+1.318$ e) of the other $z_{\mathsf{n}}$ (Zn3A, Zn3B and $Z n3C^{\prime}$ ions. In free propargylic amines, $\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{a}}$ and ${\mathsf{C}}_{\mathsf{b}}$ carry markedly different charges with $+0.209$ and $-0.586\ \textdegree$ , respectively. After the propargylic amine bonding with Zn3, $\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{a}}$ and ${\mathsf C}_{\mathsf b}$ are charged by $+0.116$ and - 0.477 e, indicating that the change of charge population for $C_{\mathsf{a}}\equiv\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{b}}$ bond should originate from the catalytic effect of zinc ions. Furthermore, for the $C_{\mathsf{a}}\equiv\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{b}}$ part, the $C_{a}-H_{a}$ bond is stretched from 1.069 to $1.075\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , and the $H_{2}-C_{2}\cdot\equiv C_{b}$ bond angle changes from nearly linear configuration (calculated to be $179.5^{\circ})$ in free molecule to bent configuration with bond angle of $156.5^{\circ}$ , indicating effective activation of $C_{\mathsf{a}}\equiv\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{b}}$ bond and formation of $Z n3–C_{a}$ bond.25 Therefore, these results are consistent with the $^1\mathsf{H}$ signal of $C\equiv C-H$ in NMR spectroscopy and characteristic peaks in FTIR. 3) $Z n(11)$ ions can efficiently activate $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ by coordination interaction and form quite short Zn-O bonds (Zn3- $\mathsf{O}_{\mathsf{a}}$ 1.983, $Z n4\mathsf{C}\mathsf{-O}_{\mathsf{b}}2.040$ and $Z n4\mathsf{G-O_{a}}2.356\mathsf{A})$ . The charges of Zn3, $Z n4C$ and $Z n4G$ are elevated to $+1.265$ , $+1.313$ and $+1.300$ e after combining with $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , and the activated $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ is negatively charged by -0.389 e. In transition state structure TS1, Zn-O bond length of $Z n3–O_{\mathsf{a}}$ , $Z n4C-O_{\mathrm{a}}$ and $Z n4G-O_{\mathsf{a}}$ become 2.415, 2.722 and $2.999\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ , respectively (Figure 4). In TS1, the $0_{\mathsf{a}^{-}}\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{c}^{-}}\mathsf{O}_{\mathsf{b}}$ bond angle of 167.2 significantly deviates from $180^{\circ}$ , suggesting $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ has been effectively activated by Zn ions. Another activation method is that TBD and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ can form TBD- $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adduct (Figure S20),26 releasing energy of $0.470\ \mathsf{e V}$ , but due to the large volume and spatial inconvenience, it is hard for TBD$\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ to further react with substrates in the nanocages of compound 1. 4) ${\sf N}_{\sf a}$ atom in propargyl amine attacks $\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{c}}$ atom of the activated $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , forming structure S3 with $N_{a}-C_{c}$ covalent bond of $1.362\mathsf{A}.$ , which is much shorter than the corresponding distance of $2.503\AA$ in TS1. Compared with TS1, $0_{\mathsf{a}^{-}}\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{c}^{-}}\mathsf{O}_{\mathsf{b}}$ bond angle reduces to 121.9 from $167.2^{\circ}$ , and $\mathrm{O}_{\mathsf{a}}\mathrm{=C}_{\mathsf{c}},$ $C_{\mathsf{c}}{=}{\mathsf{O}}_{\mathsf{b}}$ bond length are elongated from $1.183/1.172$ to $1.294/1.279$ Å in S3 (Figure S21). The formation of structure S3 leads to energy release of $0.375~{\mathsf{e V}}$ , and the corresponding activation energy is calculated to be 0.737 eV. Simultaneously, Hb atom on $\mathsf{N}_{\mathsf{a}}$ is transferred to N3C atom of most adjacent tetrazole ring with $N3C-H_{b}$ bond length of $1.074\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , and N3C is more negatively charged from -0.113 to -0.314 e during hydrogen migration, indicating the effective bonding interaction between $H_{b}$ and N3C. The cyclization reaction of S3 results in generation of structure S4 (Figure S22) with energy release of $0.843~\mathsf{e V}$ and small activation barrier $0.132~\mathsf{e V}$ (TS2 is shown in Figure S23). Due to the cycloaddition of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , the $C_{\mathsf{a}}\equiv\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{b}}$ bond is destroyed into $C_{a}=C_{b}$ bond, and $Z n3–C_{a}$ distance is shortened to 1.937 Å. $C_{\mathsf{a}^{-}}\mathsf C_{\mathsf{b}}$ distance is lengthened to $1.322\tt{A}$ , and the $H_{2}-C_{2}\equiv C_{b}$ bond angle reduces to $123.2^{\circ}$ , indicating the essential change and significant activation of $C_{\mathsf{a}}\equiv\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{b}}$ bond. Additionally, $\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{a}}/\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{b}}$ carries charges of $-0.096/+0.191\texttt{e}$ , and are opposite in sign to charges at ground state, suggesting the nature of $C_{a}=C_{b}$ bond alters significantly during cycloaddition reaction. 5) The Hb on N3C atom spontaneously migrates to $\mathsf{N}10\mathsf{\Omega}\mathrm{of}$ the nearest tetrazole ring, giving the configuration S5 (Figure S24). In S5, the $N10-H_{6}$ bond length is $1.050\ \mathsf{A},$ and the negative charge of N10 increases from -0.086 to -0.201 e, indicating relatively strong interaction between N10 and $H_{b}$ , and providing foundation for the hydrogen migration reaction between the two adjacent tetrazoles. 6) The $H_{b}$ on N10 further transfers to $\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{a}}$ with $C_{a}-H_{b}$ bond length of $1.089\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , forming S6 (Figure S25). The process for generating S6 is exothermic by $0.419\mathsf{e V}$ with small activation energy barrier of $0.495~\mathsf{e V}$ , resulting in the elevation of Zn3 charge in S6 and the cleavage of $Z n3-C a/C_{b}$ bonds due to the enhanced ionic character. Largely different from the reactants and intermediate states, both $\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{a}}$ and $c_{\flat}$ in S6 are positively charged by $+0.171$ and $+0.243\ \in$ , respectively. The transition state structure (TS3) for transformation from S5 to S6 is shown in Figure S26. The product (TM) desorbs from catalyst 1 endothermically by 0.685 eV. And the energy profiles for catalytic processes are summarized in Scheme 2a. Moreover, to further compare all reported cyclization reactions of propargylic amines catalyzed by MOFs, the different interactions for the substrate activation are analyzed and summarized in Supporting Information.
The projected density of states of compound 1 are depicted in Figures S27 and S28. The $C_{\mathsf{a}}\equiv\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{b}}$ bond in S2 shows unoccupied states between $^{+2}$ and $+3\in V$ , while $z_{\mathsf{n3}}$ in S2 displays similar peaks to those of $C_{\mathsf{a}}\equiv\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{b}}$ bond at the same energy level. For propargyl amines at ground state (S0), $C_{\mathsf{a}}\equiv\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{b}}$ bond shows discrete peaks, while $C_{\mathsf{a}}\equiv\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{b}}$ bonds in S2-S6 exhibit continuous electronic states, indicating the unsaturated $\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{a}}/\mathsf C_{\mathsf{b}}$ can form effective bonding interaction with $z_{\mathsf{n3}}$ . From S2 to S3, the lowest unoccupied states of $C_{\mathsf{a}}\equiv\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{b}}$ and Zn3 shift to $2.9~\mathsf{e V}$ . From S3 to S4, due to the formation of $C_{a}=C_{b}$ bond, the electrons in highest occupied states increase around $-0.15\ \mathsf{e V},$ and obvious occupied states around $-0.15\ \mathsf{e V}$ emerge for Zn3 active site. After the $C_{a}=C_{b}$ bond desorbs from compound 1, the electronic states of $C_{a}=C_{b}$ bond do not hybrid with characteristic peaks of $z_{\mathsf{n3}}$ , and the peaks of $C_{a}=C_{b}$ bond around $-1.2\tt e V$ are attenuated. The electronic properties of related reactants, intermediates and products can help to understand the basic physics for the reaction mechanism and crucial role of catalyst 1.
DFT calculations reveals that the charge population of nitrogen atoms in various additives as cocatalysts has great influence on the catalytic yield (NBO electric charges are shown in Table S4). The smaller the charge density of nitrogen atoms is, the lower the basicity of additives. The secondary amine nitrogen atoms of DABCO with least charge value may possess weak ability to grasp hydrogen. In addition, according to the “like dissolves like” principle, the solubility of nonpolar DABCO can be weaker than that of other additives. Generally, the charge population of nitrogen atoms can be ordered in TMG/TBD $>$ DBU $\gg$ DABCO, which can well explain the order of experimental reactivity (TMG/TBD $>$ DBU $\gg$ DABCO).
Cycloaddition reaction energies between substrate molecules and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ are obtained by theoretic calculations (Scheme 2b). The change tendency is consistent with the change of the experimental results. The volume of reactants played a major role in the catalytic process. For the bulky substrate molecule 1i (Table S5), although the cycloaddition of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ is thermo-dynamically favorable, the yield of the corresponding 2-oxazolidinone greatly reduces by the mass transfer resistance in nanopores and the steric hindrance among reactants.
## Conclusion
In summary, a unique 3D tetrazole-bridged MOF assembled by nanoscopic and lantern-like [Zn116] cages was successfully synthesized, which represents the highest nuclearity nanocages assembled by Zn-clusters as building blocks to date. Furthermore, the cage-based MOF exhibits promising application in efficiently and circularly catalyzing carboxylative cyclization of propargylic amines with $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ to exclusively afford various 2-oxazolidinones under mild conditions. More importantly, it is the first eco-friendly noble-metal-free MOFs catalyst for the cyclization of propargylic amines with $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ . The reaction mechanism for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ fixation process is proposed by DFT calculations, uncovering that zinc-clusters play an important role in activating $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and $C\equiv0$ bonds of propargylic amines, and which is consistent with the result monitored by NMR and FTIR spectroscopy. This work was hoped to open a wide space in accelerating the chemical conversion of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ catalyzed by ecofriendly noble-metal-free MOFs catalysts.
